From: Alex Konstantinov <alex.konstantinov@stfc.ac.uk>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 07:44:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] test(#72): discriminating maintained-entrypoint control
 for STAR immutability

Control 8 in tools/test_ci_fail_closed.py, exercising the real maintained
entrypoints rather than stubs.

  8A  baseline: the unmutated generator accepts the committed STAR and leaves it
      byte-identical. Without it, the mutation cases could pass against a
      generator that refuses everything.
  8B  three optics mutations -- pixel size, voltage and movie reference --
      each applied to a copy of the maintained generator, not a handwritten
      stand-in, so reverting the production policy makes these fail. Each
      asserts a nonzero exit, rejection specifically for the STAR, that the
      movie-digest check was NOT the detector (the mutation moves no pixel), and
      that the committed STAR is unmodified on disk.
  8C  the bypass path: CI runs the gates with --no-regenerate, so the verifier
      must independently reject a STAR tampered on disk. Asserts a clean
      baseline first, then the tampered case, on the specific
      "STAR input hash MISMATCH" reason.
  8D  a manifest with star_sha256 removed must fail schema validation rather
      than silently skipping the STAR check.

Controls 5 and 6 now stage the committed .star into their sandboxes alongside
the truth JSON, because canonical mode no longer writes it.

Canonical movie and ground-truth checks are unchanged, and the test-registration
union is untouched: this adds cases inside the existing CiFailClosedControls
test, so the required inventory stays at 17.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
